config ARCH_POSIX_FUZZ_IRQ
	int "OS interrupt via which to deliver fuzz cases"
	default 31
	help
	  In this test, new fuzz cases are delivered to Zephyr
	  via interrupts.  The IRQ number is arbitrary: any line the
	  board is not otherwise using works, since it is only the
	  delivery mechanism for handing the fuzz buffer into the
	  simulated OS.  The IRQ should be otherwise unused, but can
	  be any value desired by the app.

config ARCH_POSIX_FUZZ_TICKS
	int "Ticks to allow for fuzz case processing"
	default 10
	help
	  Fuzz interrupts are delivered, from the perspective of the
	  OS, at a steady cadence in simulated time.  The ISR only
	  signals a semaphore; the default of 10 ticks is the simulated
	  time nsi_exec_for() grants per iteration for that handoff to
	  reach main()'s thread AND for the full packet-draining loop
	  there to complete before the next fuzz case is delivered.
	  Too few ticks silently truncates processing rather than
	  failing loudly: the fuzzer keeps reporting steady (or even
	  rising) exec/s while quietly exercising less code per
	  iteration.  If a harness change makes per-iteration work
	  longer, watch for falling coverage alongside rising exec/s as
	  the symptom that this value needs raising.

source "Kconfig.zephyr"
